hi there steve goffman here  um today i want to talk about  something that was trigger triggered by  a tweet that i saw because i followed it  from people on twitter and it has to do  with with um  reading and developing reading skills  developing language skills  and the realization that  learning languages even improving in our  own language  uh is what i would call a very warm and  fuzzy process  uh  so many things are involved emotional  things  and and therefore  sometimes the best intentioned  strategies on the part of teachers to  set specific goals to test people for  specific skills to teach specific skills  these things in fact are  counterproductive  so  what triggered this was an article that  again this is a gentleman that i follow  on twitter called daniel willingham or  willingham who writes on  education as an american  expert  and was one of the first people to  debunk  the sort of learning styles theory which  was all the rage for a while that some  people are  you know visual learners and  can kinetic learners or  oral learners and stuff  all of which is not true  and all of these things were taught to  teachers and stuff and essentially  that's not true we may have preferences  but we all learn the same way  and  and that is now increasingly accepted  anyway he has here uh a tweet which says  why american students haven't gotten  better at reading in the past 20 years  and i'm going to post a link to the  article in the description box and the  gist of it is that the reason they don't  is because the teachers try and teach  reading skills  and then test on these reading skills  when in fact the ability to read  is very much determined by how much  knowledge we have about the subject  and that if you let kids read widely on  things of interest to them  and if you have to test them which i  doubt but if you have to test them at  least test them on something that they  have been reading about  don't test their theoretical ability to  read  based on some  uh  content that is totally irrelevant to  them so there again it's a little bit  warm and fuzzy how do we improve in  reading we improve in reading by reading  more  uh but the general knowledge that we  have which we might have acquired  through reading which might have  acquired through experience from our  parents through spears it appears rather  that this or general level of knowledge  is going to influence how well we do  just as much as uh  any specific you know reading  instructional  uh strategies in the classroom and the  proof is that in the us american  students haven't gotten better at  reading and i often mention literacy  because it is a big issue there's still  a good 20 30 percent of the population  in most developed countries that have  trouble reading  well now how does this relate back to  language learning  you know i thought about this you know  my approach to arabic to greek to the  languages that i've been learning  recently is to jump right in listening  to stories  not worrying too much about specific  aspects of grammar occasionally checking  uh to see  some explanation of what i have already  experienced  uh  and so that uh you know again it's the  same idea i think one that i've said  this before one of the problems that  that i see with many language learners  is that they are too  methodical they want to master the  basics they're going to learn  the most important words first they're  going to set themselves deliberate goals  and so forth and i think this is largely  counterproductive we have in the case of  canada for example  uh  you know i saw a statistic that outside  of quebec 6.6 of the  english-speaking canadians  speak french 6.6  that's despite the fact that all of them  have 10 years however many years of  language instruction they are tested  every year they get tests during the  year they are taught specific rules of  grammar and at the end of the 10 years  they are incapable of speaking  and don't understand french so this  deliberate approach really for the vast  majority of those people doesn't work  because there are many fuzzy  elements that affect our ability ability  to learn i.e  our interest  our motivation  our opportunities to use the language so  there's all these other fuzzy things  and uh  you know  we have to  um  kind of  sometimes force ourselves into a  language in order to discover certain  things that we might get warm and fuzzy  about  and yes i do measure and we measure it  link how many words i know how many  words i've read  but all of these goals those are not so  much goals  that you know um  i sense that if i achieve this goal like  i have said when i get to 3000 words in  arabic known words i'm going to start  speaking  but really all of the things that we  measure words known words read and stuff  is mostly a measurement of our level of  activity  and the reason that i'm able to  you know increase my known words total  increase my number of words read is  because i have found content of interest  at least sufficient interest  all right right now my mini stories are  no longer very interesting to me but at  least it is content  what i have done with asimil where i  found it very difficult to go through  their book  i've actually had someone transcribe  these so that i can learn them at link  and now i'm actually quite motivated to  go through these stores where i at least  have a chance of of hearing the words  word for word reading them and so forth  and so on  but at the same time i've started to try  to pick my way through al jazeera where  there again there's content of interest  so i'm kind of moving back and forth  between  uh  easier content where i'm trying to  ingrain certain words and phrases and  more difficult content which is  challenging me  and as long as i find that that process  gives me a sense of satisfaction then  you know i'm not too too worried about  any specific goals  uh any specific  grammatical points anything that's very  you know  grounded in in you know master the  basics i just kind of want to enjoy the  process and if i enjoy the process and  i'm picking things up picking up words  picking up some bits of knowledge you  know one of the things we're going to do  with our with my arabic stories is i  have i'm in touch with people in  jordan and syria to get some 11  arabic versions of these stories done so  that i can at least understand how that  language you know those regional uh  versions of arabic work so it's just a  gradual process of involving involving  myself with the language  and so  you know to me language learning and i  think the most successful language  learners or maybe i shouldn't say that  maybe there are people who are very  deliberate step by step  uh learn the most frequent words first  and master the grammar first maybe those  people exist but i'm not that way  and i think for a lot of people and if  we refer back to this article about  reading  they have found that if you can make it  interesting for people if our people are  able to sort of gradually  you know accumulate some experience and  poke around and find things of interest  and they're given that freedom rather  than being tested  rather than being asked questions  rather than being taught to critically  think now even there for example these  teachers love to try to teach critical  thinking  critical thinking the ability to think  critically is based on relevant  knowledge if you have knowledge then you  can think critically there's no you know  uh objective ability to think critically  about a subject that you know nothing  about  so  language whether it be your own language  reading skills  or ability to use the language or  learning you know second third fourth  languages it is a fuzzy warm vague kind  of thing and we should push ourselves  forward  into things of interest and not  focus so much on trying to master  you know the first 10 chapters  but just move on and just let them let  the language wash over us  and in the end we'll be further ahead  so i don't know if this makes any sense  but uh that's kind of the what was  running through my mind after i saw that  tweet and i'll leave a  a link to that um article in the  description box oh and uh yeah one other  thing speaking of things being vague and  fuzzy is i spoke russian after the last  video and there were some people who  kindly pointed out that i had many  mistakes which i was also aware of after  i watched the video but i didn't  practice for it i just put it out there  and we can make mistakes and the more  we speak and if i were to be with  russian people for a week or two  speaking i start to pick up on it again  in sort of a warm and fuzzy process  maybe looking a few things up in a  grammar book but you do need that degree  of  again involvement in order to maintain a  certain level in your language but i  wanted to show just what it was after  essentially uh  no activity in russian for a couple of  years and that's what it is and i will  speak in another language at some point  but i've run a bit long today so i won't  be doing that today thanks for listening  [Music]  you

